          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The prose images give the field material operations. The fish attempts to describe the water in which it lives: every distinction is sustained by what it tries to place before itself — Draft 3's faithful definition succeeds by acknowledging the limit at which its subject cannot become one more bounded thing. The definition becomes cage or window: both need the frame; the window keeps a definite frame while admitting what exceeds it (C21's living symbol). The kaleidoscope rearranges relations without licensing every description: a word becomes a local orbital centre, centralising and",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "decentring both belonging to the authored image; it returns-to Vāk. The Necker cube admits a change of orientation; the context holds the readings in relation rather than adding a fourth competing face. Centres without a final circumference remove the demand for a last enclosing spectator (A26). The broken token: keeping the fracture lets relation be authenticated across absence — one half cannot announce itself the whole (A23). The erased taijitu dots: one-dot erases the retained other, the outer form of distinction kept while the reciprocal relation that made it generative disappears (A13).\n\nOccurrences: the fish and the definition (Draft 3, A01); the cage/window (C21); the orbital word (A06); the cube's context (A04, A22); the kept counterpart (A23); the erased dots (A13).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
